Author the **Thunderhead broadside**, a capital-ship main-gun sound effect, using
only the audio tool.

It is the **main-gun broadside** for *Thunderhead*, a naval fleet-command game —
the heavy report a player's battleship fires when its main battery salvos a target.
You are authoring the *sound*, a single stereo clip, by layering samples from the
`combat-core` pack.

## How the tool works

The only way to shape sound is the `sfx-sample` binary already on your `PATH`. It
is the sole channel: you build the clip by calling it **one operation at a time**,
and the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the `.wav` on disk. Audio produced any
other way is discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

Unlike a drawing tool, `sfx-sample` does **not** re-render after every call.
Rendering is a separate step: run `sfx-sample render` to mix your recorded
operations down and draw the current **waveform + spectrogram** to `waveform.png`.
You cannot hear your clip, so **read `waveform.png` after you render** to judge the
envelope and spectral content and decide what to do next.

You are mixing over a baked **sample library** — the `combat-core` pack. Because you
cannot audition audio, browse it by metadata: `sfx-sample list-samples` (optionally
`--tag <tag>`) lists the clips, and `sfx-sample sample-info --name <name>` reads one
clip's tags, duration, and description. Do that **first**, then place layers.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
sfx-sample add-sample --name cannon_body_heavy --t 0 --gain -1
```

Run `sfx-sample --help` to list every operation and `sfx-sample <operation> --help`
for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. A
seeded config, an empty log, and the library are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the broadside up operation by operation — browse the library, layer the
concussion, sub boom, barrel ring, and debris tail, time and balance them, then
`render` and read `waveform.png` — until the clip matches the brief. Keep the whole
clip within the **four-second** cap and make sure it is not silent. When you are
satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json` is your finished submission.
